<br><br><br><table border="0" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFA8D3" width="100%"><tr><td>
<table border="0" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFA8D3" width="100%"><tr><td>
<table border="0" cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFA8D3" width="100%"><tr><td align="left">
<font class="storytitle" color="#FFFFFF"><b>Dinner Yes, Madonna No</b></font>
</td></tr></table></td></tr></table>

<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=20"><img src="images/topics/live.gif" border="0" Alt="Live" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><P><BR>June 11, 2004<BR><BR><B>Terry to host dinner, skip Madonna concert</B><BR><BR>BY LORI NITSCHKE <BR><BR><STRONG>Source: WORLD-HERALD BUREAU</STRONG><BR><BR>WASHINGTON - After Democrats and some conservatives criticized plans to raise campaign funds at a Madonna concert, U.S. Rep. Lee Terry's campaign announced Thursday that he would not attend the Monday concert.<BR><BR>Instead, he will host a dinner for contributors before the event.<BR><BR>Campaign Manager Dave Boomer said the Omaha Republican made the decision because "this generated an extreme amount of controversy and was conflicting with our ability to continue to promote his positive message and accomplishments."<BR><BR>Details, such as where the dinner would be, had not been worked out. Staff members were trying to find an available restaurant near the concert site so those contributing to Terry's campaign could use their tickets, if they wished.<BR><BR>Tickets to the show, part of Madonna's "Re-Invention World Tour," are sold out.<BR><BR>Terry leased a block of prime tickets, then sold them to donors for a profit - for $1,500 per individual or $2,500 per couple - to raise money for the campaign.<BR><BR>Terry's campaign will not ask for the tickets back. It expects to raise $20,000 from the event. Typically, such events are attended by lobbyists.<BR><BR>Raising campaign funds by that method is not unheard of. Several Republican congressmen are planning to do just that for an Eric Clapton concert.<BR><BR>But Terry's decision to host his event at the Madonna concert raised eyebrows because of the pop star's controversial past - she offended Christian groups by her use of religious icons in a music video, she wrote a book on sex and, more recently, she kissed fellow singer Britney Spears at the MTV Music Video Awards.<BR><BR>Terry has criticized the graphic nature of some television shows. Earlier this year, he asked the Federal Communications Commission to crack down on broadcasters who air "obscene, indecent or profane" material in the wake of the Janet Jackson Super Bowl furor.<BR><BR>Although Boomer said Terry was not available for comment Thursday, earlier in the week the congressman said he had scheduled the fund-raiser because "I like live music and I admit it, I like Madonna."<BR><BR>Terry, who graduated from high school in 1980, said he was a fan of her early work, especially songs such as "Holiday."<BR><BR>Terry said he had previously hosted fund-raisers at Bruce Springsteen and Eagles concerts. He said he considered canceling the Madonna event early on because of the risqué nature of her concert tour, but he decided to forge ahead when all his tickets were sold.<BR><BR>In addition, he said "there's a difference to me between what's shown using public airwaves and what's shown at a concert." </P>
<P><STRONG><EM>Thanks to our friend Slyguy for bringing this to our attention.</EM></STRONG></P>
</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


